AB  - To evaluate the added benefit of integrating features from pre-treatment MRI (radiomics) and digitized post-surgical pathology slides (pathomics) in prostate cancer (PCa) patients for prognosticating outcomes post radical-prostatectomy (RP) including a) rising prostate specific antigen (PSA), and b) extraprostatic-extension (EPE). Multi-institutional data (N = 58) of PCa patients who underwent pre-treatment 3-T MRI prior to RP were included in this retrospective study. Radiomic and pathomic features were extracted from PCa regions on MRI and RP specimens delineated by expert clinicians. On training set (D1, N = 44), Cox Proportional-Hazards models M, Mand Mwere trained using radiomics, pathomics, and their combination, respectively, to prognosticate rising PSA (PSA > 0.03 ng/mL). Top features from Mwere used to train a model to predict EPE on D1 and test on external dataset (D2, N = 14). C-index, Kalplan-Meier curves were used for survival analysis, and area under ROC (AUC) was used for EPE. Mwas compared with the existing post-treatment risk-calculator, CAPRA (M). Patients had median follow-up of 34 months. M(c-index = 0.685 ± 0.05) significantly outperformed M(c-index = 0.646 ± 0.05), M(c-index = 0.631 ± 0.06) and M(c-index = 0.601 ± 0.071) (p < 0.0001). Cross-validated Kaplan-Meier curves showed significant separation among risk groups for rising PSA for M(p < 0.005, Hazard Ratio (HR) = 11.36) as compared to M(p = 0.64, HR = 1.33), M(p = 0.19, HR = 2.82) and M(p = 0.10, HR = 3.05). Integrated radio-pathomic model M(AUC = 0.80) outperformed M(AUC = 0.57) and M(AUC = 0.76) in predicting EPE on external-data (D2). Results from this preliminary study suggest that a combination of radiomic and pathomic features can better predict post-surgical outcomes (rising PSA and EPE) compared to either of them individually as well as extant prognostic nomogram (CAPRA).
