AB  - <h4>Background: </h4> Clear cell renal cell carcinoma (ccRCC) was one of the most common cancers in in genitourinary system. Pyroprosis was a process of programmed cell death and pyroprosis modification pattern has been found significant impact on the occurrence and development in ccRCC. However, the mechanism and potential roles of pyroprosis modification pattern in ccRCC remained unclear. Methods There were 740 patients from from the Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) and the Gene-Expression Omnibus (GEO) datasets. Unsupervised algorithm was employed to classify patients into different groups and Least absolute shrinkage and selection operator (LASSO) was utilized to establish the risk score. The prognosis model was then constructed by collaborating the patients’ characteristics via plotting nomogram and the performance of our model was evaluated by receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve. Results Two different PRG clusters were established and there were 578 differentially expressed genes (DEGs) were extracted from the two PRG clusters. Patients were divided into two PRG gene clusters according to their expression of extracted DEGs afterwards. Ten significant DEGs were screened between two PRG gene clusters via LASSO and their coffecients were calculated to establish the risk score. The ROC curve of disease-free survival (DFS) for 1 year, 3 years and 5 year in testing cohort were 0.670, 0.636 and 0.651, respectively. Patients in low risk score group were found more sensitive to most chemotherapeutic drugs. Conclusion In this study, prognosis modification pattern was identified a non-negligible role in ccRCC. A prognosis model was established to estimate the prognosis of patients with ccRCC and the evaluation for drug sensitive can guide more effective chemotherapy strategies.
